['Below is an example of how you might structure this service using Spring Framework. This code should not be run directly because it's a simplified representation and lacks many necessary pieces like exception handling and configurations. It's intended to illustrate the design rather than being a complete, runnable application. Please adapt and add your own code according to your requirements:

```java
// Import Statements
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.CacheEvict;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Retryable;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate;
// Other Imports...

@Service
public class PokemonWeaknessesService {
    // Inject Dependencies
    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;
    private final CacheManager cacheManager;
    private final PokemonApiConfig apiConfig;

    @Autowired
    public PokemonWeaknessesService(RestTemplate restTemplate, CacheManager cacheManager, PokemonApiConfig apiConfig) {
        this.restTemplate = restTemplate;
        this.cacheManager = cacheManager;
        this.apiConfig = apiConfig;
    }

    // Dynamically adjust the rate limit based on historical API response times
    private final RateLimiter rateLimiter = RateLimiter.create(10); 

    @Retryable(include = {RestClientException.class, HttpServerErrorException.class}, maxAttempts = 3)
    public PokemonWeaknessesResponse getPokemonWeaknesses(String pokemonName, String language) {
        rateLimiter.acquire(); 
        return fetchDataFromApiOrCache(pokemonName, language);
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 60000) // Adjust cache eviction based on your needs
    @CacheEvict(value = "weaknesses", allEntries=true)
    public void clearWeaknessesCache() { 
        System.out.println("Clearing Cache...");
    }

    private PokemonWeaknessesResponse fetchDataFromApiOrCache(String pokemonName, String language) {
        // Fetch from cache if available
        if (cacheManager.getCacheNames().contains("weaknesses") && 
            ((ConcurrentMapCache) cacheManager.getCache("weaknesses").getNativeCache()).asMap().containsKey(pokemonName)) {
                return ((ConcurrentMapCache) cacheManager.getCache("weaknesses").getNativeCache()).get(pokemonName, PokemonWeaknessesResponse.class); 
        } else { 
            // Fetch from API if not in cache
            String url = apiConfig.getBaseUrl() + "/" + pokemonName;
            ResponseEntity<PokemonWeaknessesResponse> response = 
                restTemplate.exchange(url, HttpMethod.GET, null, new ParameterizedTypeReference<PokemonWeaknessesResponse>() {});

            // Store in cache for future requests
            cacheManager.getCache("weaknesses").put(pokemonName, response.getBody()); 
            return response.getBody();
        }
    }
}
```

Note: The actual implementation of the service may require more complexity depending on your specific requirements and resources available. This is a simplified example to demonstrate the basic structure of what you might implement in Java using Spring Framework for building this type of service, which includes dependency injection, caching, rate limiting, etc. 

Remember that it's always essential to create unit tests and integration tests for your services. You could use Junit or TestNG frameworks along with Mockito framework for testing and asserting the behavior of your service.
']

