["Here is an example of how you could implement this JavaScript module. Please note that actual implementation would be more complex as it would need to deal with many edge cases, including handling backpressure in data collection from sources which are faster than your cache can handle the data etc. Also, this code assumes some level of familiarity with Promises and async/await.

```javascript
class StreamingDataProcessor {
  constructor(config) {
    this._cache = config.cache;
    this._sources = config.sources || [];
    this._evictionPolicy = config.evictionPolicy || new LRUEvictionPolicy();
  }

  async fetchData() {
    const promises = this._sources.map(async (source) => {
      try {
        const data = await source.getData();
        this._cache.store(data);
        this._evictionPolicy.touch(data);
      } catch (error) {
        console.log('Error fetching data from source:', error);
      }
    });

    return Promise.allSettled(promises);
  }

  pause() {
    this._sources = [];
  }

  resume(config) {
    if (config && config.sources) {
      this._sources = [...this._sources, ...config.sources];
    }
  }

  purgeCache() {
    this._evictionPolicy.purge();
  }
}

class LRUEvictionPolicy {
  constructor(capacity = 10) {
    this._cache = new Map();
    this._usageHistory = [];
    this._capacity = capacity;
  }

  touch(data) {
    if (this._cache.has(data)) {
      // Move data to the end of usage history
      const index = this._usageHistory.indexOf(data);
      this._usageHistory.push(this._usageHistory.splice(index, 1)[0]);
    } else {
      if (this._cache.size === this._capacity) {
        // Remove least recently used data
        const lru = this._usageHistory.shift();
        this._cache.delete(lru);
      }

      this._cache.set(data, true);
      this._usageHistory.push(data);
    }
  }

  purge() {
    this._cache = new Map();
    this._usageHistory = [];
  }
}
```
In this code snippet:
1. We define a StreamingDataProcessor class that takes in a configuration object containing cache, sources of streaming data and eviction policy for managing cache size.
2. The fetchData method fetches data from all the sources concurrently using Promise.allSettled. It logs any errors but keeps processing other sources even if one fails.
3. To pause data collection, it clears the sources array. To resume, it adds new sources to existing ones.
4. The purgeCache method is part of StreamingDataProcessor and calls the purge method on the eviction policy.
5. We define a LRUEvictionPolicy class that uses a Map for storing data and an usageHistory array to keep track of recently used items. When we touch an item (get or set), it moves the item to the end of the usage history, keeping it fresh in cache. If cache is full, it removes the least recently used item from both Map and usage history before adding new one.
6. This code needs to be integrated into a mobile application framework and tested accordingly. For example, if we are using React Native, this module could be easily exported as a native module and imported in JavaScript files of our app.
"]

