['Here is a basic implementation of your requirements. This is an example using Spring Boot's task executor feature which allows managing background tasks with ease. It uses `ExecutorService` to manage multiple tasks running concurrently. 

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskExecutor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

import javax.annotation.PreDestroy;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

@Service
public class BackgroundProcessingService {
    private final Map<String, ScheduledFuture> tasks = new HashMap<>();
    @Autowired
    private HealthCheckMonitor monitor;
    @Autowired
    private Logger logger;

    public void start(Runnable task, String name, int interval) {
        stop(name); // if already exists then stop it first before starting again.
        ScheduledFuture<?> future = executor.scheduleAtFixedRate(task, interval);
        tasks.put(name, future);
    }

    public void stop(String name) {
        Optional.ofNullable(tasks.get(name)).ifPresent(future -> future.cancel(true));
        tasks.remove(name);
    }

    @PreDestroy
    public void destroy() {
        tasks.keySet().forEach(this::stop);
    }
}
```

A runnable task could be like this:
```java
public class SomeBusinessFunctionTask implements Runnable {
    @Autowired
    private MonitoringSystemClient monitoringSystemClient;

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            // Do business logic here.
            // If necessary, notify monitoring system about metrics and health status updates.
            monitoringSystemClient.notifyMetrics(metrics); 

            if (networkError) {
                throw new NetworkException("Network error occurred"); 
            }
        } catch (Throwable t) {
            // Log the exception, notify monitoring system about error.
            logger.error("Task failed", t); 
            monitoringSystemClient.notifyFailure(t);
