['Below is a high-level overview and code snippet for this problem in Java using Spring Boot, Guava Retry library, Resilience4J CircuitBreaker, and JUnit for testing. This example uses HttpUrlConnection to call remote REST APIs and Files API to manage local files but actual implementation would vary according to specific requirements like the external APIs used, commands executed locally etc.

```Java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import com.google.common.util.concurrent.*;
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.slf4j.*;

@Service
public class RestAPIService {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(RestAPIService.class);
    // Assume this to be the unreliable API
    private static final String UNRELIABLE_API = "http://unreliable-api.com"; 

    @Autowired
    CommandExecutor commandExecutor;

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "externalApi", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    public boolean deleteFileAndExecuteCommands(String fileName) {
        if (checkRemoteFileExistence(fileName)) {
            Retryer<Boolean> retryer = RetryerBuilder.<Boolean>newBuilder()
                    .retryIfResult(Predicates.not(Predicates.equalTo(true)))
                    .withWaitStrategy(WaitStrategies.exponentialWait())
                    .build();
            try {
                retryer.call(() -> deleteRemoteFile(fileName));  // Retry deleting the file
            } catch (RetryException e) {
                LOGGER.error("Failed to delete file after multiple attempts", e);
                return false;
            }
        }

        // Execute local commands
        commandExecutor.executeCommands();
        return true;
    }

    public boolean checkRemoteFileExistence(String fileName) {
        try {
            HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) new URL(UNRELIABLE_API + "/" + fileName).openConnection();
            connection.setRequestMethod("HEAD");

            int responseCode = connection.getResponseCode();
            return responseCode == HttpURLConnection.HTTP_OK;
        } catch (IOException e) {
            LOGGER.error("Failed to check remote file existence", e);
            return false;
        }
    }

    public boolean deleteRemoteFile(String fileName) {
        try {
            HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) new URL(UNRELIABLE_API + "/" + fileName).openConnection();
            connection.setRequestMethod("DELETE");

            int responseCode = connection.getResponseCode();
            return responseCode == HttpURLConnection.HTTP_OK;
        } catch (IOException e) {
            LOGGER.error("Failed to delete remote file", e);
            return false;
        }
    }

    public void fallback(String fileName, Throwable t) {
        // Fallback action after circuit breaker trips
        LOGGER.warn("Circuit breaker opened for delete and execute commands due to: " + t.toString());
    }
}
``` 
Please note that this is a simplified example and actual implementation would vary significantly based on requirements like the external APIs, local commands etc. Also, proper secure management of sensitive data such as API keys or secrets should be handled appropriately in production grade applications. This includes not exposing them in logs/error messages but keeping them safely stored for use during runtime.

For unit and integration testing, Junit test classes can be written to cover all the flows including edge cases. Also, it's important to consider performance implications of different methods used and optimize accordingly based on specific requirements. 

Remember this code should be treated as a starting point and needs refactoring and improvements in many areas. This includes logging configuration, secure management of sensitive data, error handling, testing etc.
