['The following code is an example of a simple dynamic Java module that adheres to the SerializableDataProcessor interface and integrates with multiple external sources. It uses Java's inbuilt serialization mechanism for deserializing objects. It also handles exceptions by isolating faulty data streams, prompting warnings when possible without halting the application and minimizes cascading failures.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class SerializableDataProcessorImpl implements SerializableDataProcessor {
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, Object> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    @Override
    public void process(Object data) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        if (data instanceof byte[]) {
            ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream((byte[]) data);
            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais);
            Object deserializedData;

            try {
                while ((deserializedData = ois.readObject()) != null) {
                    cache.putIfAbsent(deserializedData.toString(), deserializedData);
                    processData(deserializedData);
                }
            } catch (EOFException eofe) {
                 // End of data stream is reached.
            } catch (OptionalDataException ode) {
                System.err.println("Warning: Optional Data Exception occurred while deserializing.");
                handleIncompatibleVersion(ode);
            } catch (InvalidClassException ice) {
                System.err.println("Error: Invalid Class detected while deserializing.");
                throw new RuntimeException("Cannot recover from invalid class errors.", ice);
            } finally {
                ois.close();
                bais.close();
            }
        } else {
            System.err.println("Error: Unknown data type encountered.");
        }
    }

    private void processData(Object data) {
        // Process the deserialized data as required by the application.
    }

    private void handleIncompatibleVersion(OptionalDataException ode) {
        // Handle incompatible version of serialized data here. For instance, log a warning or throw an exception.
    }
}
```
This code can be further improved and extended according to the requirements and constraints mentioned above. It's important to note that building a system like this requires careful design, testing, and performance tuning. Therefore, it is highly recommended to involve experts in your organization or engage professional services for developing such systems.
