['The task at hand is very complex and requires a deep understanding of distributed system design, concurrency control, and REST API development in C++. Here's an example for the high-level structure of your code using various components. A real implementation would require several additional components such as actual business logic, database interactions and more. 

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <memory>
#include <unordered_map>
#include "restclient-cpp/restclient.h"

class Job {
public:
    void process() {
        std::cout << "Processing job..." << std::endl;
    }
};

class JobQueue {
private:
    std::queue<std::shared_ptr<Job>> jobs_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
public:
    void add(const std::shared_ptr<Job>& job) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        jobs_.push(job);
        lock.unlock();
        cv_.notify_one();
    }

    std::shared_ptr<Job> take() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        while (jobs_.empty()) {
            cv_.wait(lock);
        }
        auto job = jobs_.front();
        jobs_.pop();
        return job;
    }
};

class Worker {
private:
    JobQueue& queue_;
public:
    Worker(JobQueue& queue) : queue_(queue) {}

    void operator() () {
        for (;;) {
            auto job = queue_.take();
            job->process();
        }
    }
};

class JobManager {
private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, RestClient::Response> responses_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
public:
    void processJob(const std::shared_ptr<Job>& job) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto response = RestClient::post("http://example.com", "text/json", "");
        responses_.emplace(job->id, response);
    }
};

int main() {
    JobQueue job_queue;
    std::vector<std::thread> workers;
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < std::thread::hardware_concurrency(); ++i) {
        workers.push_back(std::thread(Worker(job_queue)));
    }

    JobManager job_manager;

    // Create and add jobs to the queue...
    for (int i = 0; i < 10000; ++i) {
        auto job = std::make_shared<Job>();
        job_queue.add(job);

        // Process jobs concurrently on worker threads...
        job_manager.processJob(job);
    }

    for (auto& t : workers) {
        if (t.joinable()) {
            t.join();
        }
    }
}
```
This is a simple example of how you could design your system in C++ using modern C++ features such as std::thread, std::mutex, and std::condition_variable for synchronization. This code does not include error handling, logging, transformation pipelines, or other components that would be needed in a production system. Also, it assumes the use of an external library (restclient-cpp) for REST communication which needs to be installed separately. 

In a real implementation, you'd likely need more classes and functions to encapsulate different parts of your system. For example, you might have separate JobManager and Worker classes, or a TransformPipeline class that applies a series of transformations to incoming jobs. You could also use third-party libraries for things like JSON serialization/deserialization, HTTP requests, logging, etc.
']

