['Below is an example code block as per the requirements, but please note that it's not a complete solution. It only provides a starting point for your implementation. 

The code below outlines a basic approach to solving the task in Java using Fork/Join Framework and Union-Find algorithm with some considerations on performance, error handling, concurrency, scaling etc.

```Java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.apache.http.*;
import org.apache.http.client.config.*;
import org.apache.http.client.methods.*;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.*;
import org.apache.http.util.*;

public class DisjointPairsService {

    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newWorkStealingPool(); // Fork/Join Pool
    private final CloseableHttpClient httpClient;

    public DisjointPairsService() {
        RequestConfig requestConfig = RequestConfig.custom().build();
        this.httpClient = HttpClients.custom().setDefaultRequestConfig(requestConfig).build();
    }

    // External dependency validation 
    private String validateDependencyAndFetchData(String url) throws IOException {
        HttpGet request = new HttpGet(url);
        try (CloseableHttpResponse response = this.httpClient.execute(request)) {
            HttpEntity entity = response.getEntity();
            if (entity != null) {
                return EntityUtils.toString(entity);
            } else {
                throw new IOException("External API returned empty data");
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Retry with exponential backoff
            try {
                Thread.sleep((long) Math.pow(2, executor.getCompletedTaskCount()) * 1000);
                return validateDependencyAndFetchData(url);
            } catch (InterruptedException ex) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                throw new IOException("External API call interrupted", e);
            }
        }
    }

    // Concurrency and System Integration
    public Future<Integer> countDisjointPairs(List<Integer> array) {
        return executor.submit(() -> countDisjointPairsRecursive(array, 0, array.size() - 1));
    }

    // Union-Find Algorithm for disjoint pairs calculation
    private int countDisjointPairsRecursive(List<Integer> array, int start, int end) {
        if (start >= end) return 0;

        int mid = start + ((end - start) / 2);
        // Divide and Conquer: Recursively calculate on both halves of the list
        Future<Integer> leftCount = countDisjointPairs(array.subList(start, mid + 1));
        Future<Integer> rightCount = countDisjointPairs(array.subList(mid + 1, end + 1));

        return Math.max(leftCount.get(), rightCount.get()) + unionFind(array, start, mid, end);
    }

    private int unionFind(List<Integer> array, int start, int mid, int end) {
        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

        for (int i = start; i <= mid; i++) {
            if (!set.add(array.get(i))) return 0; // If not disjoint pair found
        }

        int count = 1;
        for (int i = mid + 1; i <= end; i++) {
            if (!set.add(array.get(i)) && count == 1) count--; // If already seen and this is first duplicate, decrease the count 
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```
This example uses Apache HttpClient for external dependency calls and Java's Fork/Join Framework for parallel computing. Union-Find algorithm is implemented recursively to efficiently compute disjoint pair counts. Error handling includes retries with exponential backoff. 

Note: This code does not cover performance optimization, resource management, monitoring and reporting, system extensibility etc., which would need further implementation according to the specific requirements of your project.
']

['This is quite a complex task and implementing all the requirements within this platform would be challenging. However, I can outline how you might structure your code for each of these components. Please note that actual implementation of such a service in Java with NIO Channels, integrations with an external message broker, cloud-native infrastructure, security measures, resource management, system integration, and monitoring/observability will require much more complex code which cannot be fully covered here due to its complexity and length.

1. Scalable Service using Java NIO: 
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;

public class ScalableService {
    private Selector selector;
    private InetSocketAddress remoteServerAddress;
    // Assume you have a connection pool or other mechanism to manage connections
    private ConnectionPool connectionPool = new ConnectionPool();

    public void start() throws IOException {
        this.selector = Selector.open();

        for (Connection connection : connectionPool) {
            connection.register(this.selector);
        }

        while (!Thread.interrupted()) {
            // Use select to wait for events on the sockets
            selector.select();

            Set<SelectionKey> selectedKeys = selector.selectedKeys();
            Iterator<SelectionKey> iter = selectedKeys.iterator();

            while (iter.hasNext()) {
                SelectionKey key = iter.next();

                if (key.isAcceptable()) {
                    // Handle incoming connections
                    ServerSocketChannel server = (ServerSocketChannel) key.channel();
                    SocketChannel client = server.accept();
                    connectionPool.add(new Connection(client));
                } else if (key.isReadable()) {
                    // Read data from the socket
                    Connection connection = (Connection) key.attachment();
                    connection.readDataFromSocket();
                } else if (key.isWritable()) {
                    // Write data to the socket
                    Connection connection = (Connection) key.attachment();
                    connection.writeDataToSocket();
                }

                iter.remove();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
