```java
public class AsyncMessagingQueue {
    // Define common configurations
    private static final int DEFAULT_NUMBER_OF_WORKER_THREADS = 5;
    private static final long EXPONENTIAL_BACKOFF_DELAY = 1000L; // 1 second

    private ExecutorService executorService;
    private List<AsyncMessageBroker> brokers;

    public AsyncMessagingQueue() {
        this(DEFAULT_NUMBER_OF_WORKER_THREADS);
    }

    public AsyncMessagingQueue(int numberOfWorkerThreads) {
        executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(numberOfWorkerThreads);
        brokers = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    // Register a message broker
    public void registerBroker(AsyncMessageBroker broker) {
        this.brokers.add(broker);
    }

    // Send a message
    public Future<Boolean> send(String queueName, String message) {
        return executorService.submit(() -> {
            for (AsyncMessageBroker broker : brokers) {
                try {
                    if (broker.isAvailable()) {
                        return broker.send(queueName, message);
                    }
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    // handle error and retry with exponential backoff delay
                    Thread.sleep(EXPONENTIAL_BACKOFF_DELAY);
                }
            }

            // all brokers are unavailable or failed to send the message
            return false;
        });
    }

    // Other queue management methods can be implemented in a similar way...
}
```

This is a very simplified version of what such a library might look like. In practice, you would need to implement the AsyncMessageBroker interface with specific broker implementation classes and handle more complex scenarios including error handling, authentication, caching, metrics collection etc. You will also likely need additional components for managing concurrent access to shared resources, implementing load balancing strategies, supporting different types of queues, and integrating with monitoring tools.
