['Given the complexity of this task, here is a simple outline for your `StateManager` class with some basic functionalities including plugin framework, caching strategy, error handling, and retry mechanism.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.lang.reflect.*;

interface StorageFormatPlugin {
    Object load(String filePath);
}

class Cache<K, V> {
    private final int maxSize;
    private final Map<K, V> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public Cache(int maxSize) { this.maxSize = maxSize; }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        if (cache.size() >= maxSize) {
            // Implement a policy to evict entries from the cache based on your requirements
            cache.remove(/* some entry */);
        }
        cache.put(key, value);
    }

    public V get(K key) { return cache.get(key); }
}

public class StateManager {
    private final Map<String, StorageFormatPlugin> plugins = new HashMap<>();
    private final Cache<String, Object> stateCache;

    public StateManager(int maxCacheSize) { this.stateCache = new Cache<>(maxCacheSize); }

    // Dynamically load a plugin by its class name
    public void addPlugin(String className) throws Exception {
        Class<?> clazz = Class.forName(className);
        Constructor<?> ctor = clazz.getConstructor();
        StorageFormatPlugin instance = (StorageFormatPlugin) ctor.newInstance();
        plugins.put(className, instance);
    }

    public Object loadState(String storageFormat, String filePath) throws Exception {
        StorageFormatPlugin plugin = plugins.get(storageFormat);
        if (plugin == null) { throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported storage format: " + storageFormat); }

        // Check cache first
        Object state = stateCache.get(filePath);
        if (state != null) { return state; }

        // If not in cache, load from plugin and update the cache
        state = retry(() -> plugin.load(filePath));
        stateCache.put(filePath, state);
        return state;
    }

    private Object retry(Supplier<Object> supplier) throws Exception {
        int maxRetries = 5; // Adjust as needed
        long backoffMillis = 1000; // Start with 1 second

        for (int i = 0; i < maxRetries; ++i) {
            try {
                return supplier.get();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                if (i == maxRetries - 1) throw e; // Last retry, propagate error upwards

                Thread.sleep(backoffMillis);
                backoffMillis *= 2; // Exponential backoff
            }
        }

        throw new RuntimeException("Unreachable");
    }

    public void notifyOnStateChange(StateChangeEvent event) {
        // Implement notification logic here
    }
}
```

This is a basic implementation and does not cover all the requirements in depth. You need to build on this to satisfy your needs, including error handling, concurrency control, logging integration, dynamic configuration updates etc. 

You would also need to define a concrete `StorageFormatPlugin` for each of the supported storage formats (like JSON, YAML etc). This could be as simple or as complex as you wish depending on how much control and flexibility you want over each format's loading process. You might use libraries like Jackson or SnakeYaml if your states are structured objects and require full object mapping capabilities.
