['Here is a simplified example of how you might start implementing such a system in C++. Note that this is a complex task, and it's impossible to provide the full solution here due to space limitations. This implementation assumes that the elements are integers for simplicity, but your real-world application would likely need to handle custom data types or classes.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <mutex>
#include <stdexcept>

class CustomHeap {
private:
    std::vector<int> heap;
    std::mutex mutex;
public:
    void push(const int& item) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        heap.push_back(item);
        std::push_heap(heap.begin(), heap.end());
    }

    void pop() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        if (heap.empty()) throw std::runtime_error("Heap is empty");

        std::pop_heap(heap.begin(), heap.end());
        heap.pop_back();
    }

    int top() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        if (heap.empty()) throw std::runtime_error("Heap is empty");

        return heap.front();
    }
};
```
This simple class provides `push`, `pop` and `top` operations which are thread-safe due to the use of a `std::mutex`. The operations on the vector inside these functions ensure that the vector always represents a valid heap structure after the operation completes. Note that in a real implementation, you'd want to add more robust error checking and possibly more fine-grained locking to improve performance under high contention.
