["Creating a complete Node.js microservice with all these features would be quite extensive and beyond the scope of this platform. However, I can provide you with an example showing how to create a simple logging system in Node.js using 'winston' library for multi-tenant log events handling. 

Please install required dependencies: `npm i express winston`

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const winston = require('winston');

// Initialize Express app and Winston logger
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const logger = winston.createLogger({
  transports: [new winston.transports.Console()],
});

// Middleware to log requests
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  logger.info(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next();
});

// Route for logging events
app.post('/log', (req, res) => {
  const tenant = req.body.tenant; // Assuming each log event includes the tenant ID
  const level = req.body.level || 'info'; // If not provided, default to info
  const message = req.body.message;

  logger.log({ level, message, metadata: { tenant } });
  res.status(204).send();
});

// Start the server
const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(port, () => console.log(`Listening on port ${port}...`));
```
Please note that this is a very basic example and doesn't include all of your requirements (like circuit breakers, distributed cache for log metadata, auto-scaling based on metrics, secure multi-tenancy, API design). For these features, you would need to use additional libraries or services. Also consider implementing other advanced techniques like session storage in Redis, load balancing and cost-effective long-term storage of less critical logs as per your requirements.
