```java
public abstract class DataProcessingPipeline {
    private final KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer;
    private final ExecutorService executor;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public DataProcessingPipeline(Properties properties) {
        this.consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(properties);
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Integer.parseInt(properties.getProperty("threads")));
    }

    public void run() {
        try {
            while (running) {
                ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
                for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
                    executor.submit(() -> handleRecord(record));
                }
            }
        } finally {
            consumer.close();
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        running = false;
        executor.shutdown();
    }

    protected abstract void handleRecord(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record);
}
```
In this skeleton code, `DataProcessingPipeline` is an abstract class that implements a basic Kafka consumer with a thread pool for processing records in parallel. You would create your own concrete subclass of `DataProcessingPipeline` and implement the `handleRecord` method to define how each record should be processed.

