["Here's how you can modify your function to take a string, size limit as input and return a new string with original string truncated to the specified size limit. It inserts '/' at the end if the text is truncated.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

const char* truncate_and_insert_slash(const char *str, size_t size) {
    const size_t strLen = strlen(str);  // Get the length of input string

    if (size < 1 || strLen <= size) return str;  // If limit is not exceeded or invalid, return original string

    char* newStr = malloc((strLen + 2)*sizeof(char));  // Allocate memory for the new string
    memcpy(newStr, str, (size-1)*sizeof(char));  // Copy characters from input to new string upto size limit

    newStr[size-1] = '/';  // Insert slash at end of truncated string
    newStr[size] = '\0';  // Null terminate the new string

    return newStr;
}
```
This function first checks if the size limit is less than or equal to 0 or exceeds the length of input string. If it does, then there's no need for truncation and the original string is returned as it is. Otherwise, a new memory block is allocated for storing the truncated string. The characters from the original string are copied upto the size limit into the new string and slash '/' is appended at the end.
"]

