['Here's a basic example of how you can begin implementing such a library. This does not include all the details like integrating with an external RESTful API, seamless integration with a database and other performance optimization tasks mentioned in your task as they require deep understanding of specific use-cases and system constraints which are typically not provided in this kind of brief problem description. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    void* EPS_MobilityManagement;
    void* Non_AccessStratum;
    void* S_GW_context;
} LTE_MME;

typedef struct Node {
    LTE_MME data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addMessage(LTE_MME message) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory for new node.\n");
        exit(-1);
    }

    newNode->data = message;
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

LTE_MME getMessage() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (head == NULL) {
        printf("No messages to retrieve.\n");
        exit(-1);
    }

    LTE_MME message = head->data;
    Node* oldHead = head;
    head = head->next;
    free(oldHead);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return message;
}
```
This library maintains a linked list of MME messages. It uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can add or retrieve a message at any given time, ensuring thread safety and preventing race conditions. 

Please note that the code above assumes that you have already defined `LTE_MME` type for your LTE Mobility Management Entity, `Non_AccessStratum` for Non-Access Stratum and `S_GW_context` for Serving Gateway Context messages.

