```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>

enum OperationType { READ, WRITE, TRUNCATE };

struct FileOperationDetails {
  std::string filePath;
  size_t bufferSize;
  // Add more metadata fields as required.
};

struct OPERATION_T {
  uint64_t uniqueId;
  OperationType operationType;
  FileOperationDetails details;
  std::time_t timestamp;
};

class CircularBuffer {
 public:
  explicit CircularBuffer(size_t size) : buffer_(size), head_(0), tail_(0), full_(false) {}

  void push(OPERATION_T operation) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    while (full_) {
      notFull_.wait(lock);
    }
    buffer_[head_] = operation;
    head_ = (head_ + 1) % buffer_.size();
    full_ = head_ == tail_;
  }

  OPERATION_T pop() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    while (!full_ && head_ == tail_) {
      notEmpty_.wait(lock);
    }
    auto operation = buffer_[tail_];
    full_ = false;
    tail_ = (tail_ + 1) % buffer_.size();
    return operation;
  }

 private:
  std::vector<OPERATION_T> buffer_;
  size_t head_, tail_;
  bool full_;
  std::mutex mutex_;
  std::condition_variable notEmpty_, notFull_;
};
```
This code defines a simple circular buffer with push and pop methods that block when the buffer is full or empty, respectively. The `OPERATION_T` structure represents an individual file operation, while the `CircularBuffer` class manages these operations in a thread-safe manner using locks and condition variables.

This code does not handle memory allocation failures, external API errors, or invalid operation inputs. It also lacks the high performance requirements for handling a large number of operations per second with minimal latency and memory overhead. It also doesn't include validation logic to ensure each operation is valid and properly formatted, support different file modes and attributes, or monitor file access statistics. These features would require significant additional complexity and are beyond the scope of this task.
