['Implementing this full-featured system requires extensive knowledge of C++ programming, network protocols, concurrency management, error handling, machine learning algorithms, etc., which goes beyond the scope of a single code block or even a simple console application. 

However, I can provide you with a very basic example of how you might implement a producer-consumer queue in C++ using standard library's `queue` and `thread` libraries:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <thread>

class MessageQueue {
public:
    void push(int val) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
        q.push(val);
        cv.notify_one();
    }

    int pop() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);
        while (q.empty()) {
            cv.wait(lock);
        }
        int val = q.front();
        q.pop();
        return val;
    }

private:
    std::queue<int> q;
    std::mutex m;
    std::condition_variable cv;
};

void producer(MessageQueue& q) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {
        q.push(i);
        std::cout << "Produced: " << i << "\n";
    }
}

void consumer(MessageQueue& q) {
    while (true) {
        int val = q.pop();
        std::cout << "Consumed: " << val << "\n";
    }
}

int main() {
    MessageQueue q;
    std::thread t1(producer, std::ref(q));
    std::thread t2(consumer, std::ref(q));

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a basic producer-consumer model where one thread produces items and the other consumes them. The `push` method is synchronized to prevent concurrent access, ensuring only one thread modifies the queue at a time. The consumer waits on a condition variable until it's notified by the producer that an item has been added.
']

['Here's how you might go about creating such a library using Rust with tokio and async/await. Note that this is a very simplified example and does not cover all the mentioned challenges in depth:

```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs::{self, DirEntry};
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::sync::Mutex;
use tokio::sync::Semaphore;
use tokio::task::JoinHandle;
use thiserror::Error;

pub struct DirAndEnvManager {
    env_var: String,
    dirs: Mutex<Vec<DirEntry>>,
}

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum DirAndEnvManagerError {
    #[error("invalid directory name")]
    InvalidDirectoryName,
    #[error("io error")]
    IoError(#![cfg_attr(feature = "use-std", doc = ::std::io::Error)] #[cfg_attr(feature = "use-std", doc = ::std::io::ErrorKind::Other)]),
}

impl DirAndEnvManager {
    pub async fn create_dir_and_set_env(&self, name: &str) -> Result<(), DirAndEnvManagerError> {
        let semaphore = Semaphore::new(1);
        let join_handle = tokio::spawn({
            let lock = self.dirs.lock().await;
            async move {
                let _permit = semaphore.acquire().await;
                if let Err(e) = fs::create_dir(&name) {
                    return Err(DirAndEnvManagerError::IoError(e));
                }
                lock.push(fs::read_dir(&name).unwrap());
            }
        });
        match join_handle.await {
            Ok(_) => {
                env::set_var("DIR", name)?;
                Ok(())
            },
            Err(e) => Err(DirAndEnvManagerError::IoError(e)),
        }
    }
}
```

Please note that in a real-world scenario, more details would be needed. This is a simple example just to demonstrate the idea of using tokio and async/await for concurrency handling.
