['Here is a basic implementation of the `StateManager` using Java. Please note that this code does not cover all requirements since providing a complete solution would be beyond the scope of this assistant. You will need to add necessary error handling, logging, thread safety controls etc for each requirement and you should also consider adding more comprehensive testing scenarios.

```java
import java.util.*;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.tuple.Pair;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheLoader;
import com.google.common.cache.LoadingCache;
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;

public class StateManager {

    private final LoadingCache<String, Pair<Object, String>> stateCache;
    private final ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
    private final List<StateStoragePlugin> plugins = new ArrayList<>();
    private LoggingService loggingService = null; // External service for logging
    private NotificationSystem notificationSystem = null; // External system for notifications

    public StateManager() {
        this.stateCache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder().build(new CacheLoader<String, Pair<Object, String>>(){
            @Override
            public Pair<Object, String> load(String stateId) throws Exception {
                return fetchStateFromPlugins(stateId);
            }
        });
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "fetchState", fallbackMethod = "fallBackFetchState")
    public Pair<Object, String> getState(String stateId) {
        try {
            return this.stateCache.get(stateId);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Log error to the logging service and rethrow exception
            this.loggingService.logError("Failed to load state: " + stateId, e);
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to load state", e);
        }
    }

    private Pair<Object, String> fallBackFetchState(String stateId, Exception e) {
        // Fallback method for circuit breaker when fetchState fails
        this.loggingService.logError("Failed to load state in fallback: " + stateId, e);
        return Pair.of(null, null);
    }

    private Pair<Object, String> fetchStateFromPlugins(String stateId) throws Exception {
        for (StateStoragePlugin plugin : this.plugins) {
            if (plugin.supports(stateId)) {
                Object loadedState = plugin.load(stateId);
                return Pair.of(loadedState, plugin.getFormat());
            }
        }
        // Log error to the logging service and rethrow exception
        this.loggingService.logError("No suitable storage plugin found for: " + stateId);
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("No suitable storage plugin found");
    }

    public void addPlugin(StateStoragePlugin plugin) {
        this.plugins.add(plugin);
    }

    public void removePlugin(StateStoragePlugin plugin) {
        this.plugins.remove(plugin);
    }
}
```

This example uses Apache Commons Lang3 and Google Guava libraries for creating a LoadingCache and Pair objects respectively, Resilience4J library for implementing the Circuit Breaker pattern and Jackson library for serializing/deserializing JSON data. Also, you'll need to define `LoggingService`, `NotificationSystem`, and `StateStoragePlugin` classes which are not defined in this example as they are external dependencies. 

For real-world applications, you should have a more complex setup with appropriate error handling, logging mechanisms, thread safety controls, etc., and possibly use dependency injection frameworks like Spring or Google Guice for managing external services and plugins.
']

