['This task involves several complex components and requires good understanding of Java programming, distributed systems design, cloud technologies, and continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) practices. Here's a basic implementation skeleton to guide you on how to approach this problem.

```java
import java.util.*;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.function.FailableRunnable;

public class MonitoringSystem {
    private final CloudMonitoringService cloudMonitoringService = new CloudMonitoringService();
    private final ConfigurationManagementAPI configurationManagementAPI = new ConfigurationManagementAPI();
    private final MessageBroker messageBroker = new MessageBroker();
    private final Logger logger;

    private final ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
    private ScheduledExecutorService scheduledExecutorService = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(5);

    public MonitoringSystem() {
        this.logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MonitoringSystem.class);
    }

    private void startServiceInstanceWithDelay(Service service) {
        int delay = cloudMonitoringService.fetchLoadMetrics();

        // Adjust delay based on real-time system load metrics
        scheduledExecutorService.schedule(() -> {
            FailableRunnable<Void, RuntimeException> task = 
                () -> service.start(configurationManagementAPI.fetchUpdatedConfiguration());

            try {
                RetryUtil.retry(task); // Use utility method to retry failed operations with exponential backoff
                logger.info("Service started");

            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.error("Failed to start service", e);
            }
        }, delay, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    private void stopServiceInstance(Service service) {
        FailableRunnable<Void, RuntimeException> task = () -> service.stop();

        try {
            RetryUtil.retry(task); // Use utility method to retry failed operations with exponential backoff
            logger.info("Service stopped");

        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Failed to stop service", e);
        }
    }

    private void fetchAndApplyUpdatedConfigurations() {
        FailableRunnable<Void, RuntimeException> task = () -> {
            configurationManagementAPI.fetchUpdatedConfiguration();

            // Restart services with new configurations
            serviceInstance.forEach(this::stopServiceInstance);
            serviceInstance.clear();
            startServicesWithDelay();
        };

        try {
            RetryUtil.retry(task); // Use utility method to retry failed operations with exponential backoff

        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.error("Failed to fetch and apply updated configurations", e);
        }
    }

    private void startServicesWithDelay() {
        List<Service> services = configurationManagementAPI.fetchServices();

        for(Service service : services) {
            // Start each service instance with a delay based on load metrics
            startServiceInstanceWithDelay(service);
        }
    }

    private void initialize() {
        fetchAndApplyUpdatedConfigurations();
        startServicesWithDelay();
    }
}
```
Please note that this code skeleton is a simplified version of what your system might look like. It doesn't cover all requirements such as integration with external monitoring tools or APIs, detailed logging, memory and CPU usage optimization for high-frequency service starts/stops, handling network and API availability issues with robust retry mechanisms, and extensive unit testing. You may need to split some functionality into separate classes based on the Single Responsibility Principle, and create additional methods as per your needs.
