#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char* truncate_and_insert_slash(const char* str, size_t limit) {
    if (strlen(str) <= limit) return strdup(str); // No need to truncate

    char *truncated = malloc(limit + 2); 
    if (!truncated) return NULL;

    memcpy(truncated, str, limit);
    truncated[limit] = '/';
    truncated[limit + 1] = '\0'; // Null-terminate the string

    return truncated;
}
```

This function works by first checking if the input string is already shorter than or equal to the specified limit. If it is, we simply duplicate the original string and return the new copy. 

If the length of the input string exceeds the limit, we allocate a buffer for our truncated string that includes space for the slash and null-terminator characters. We then copy as much data from the original string to our truncated one as fits within the given limit. After copying, we insert a slash at the end of the truncated string. The function finally returns a pointer to the new string or NULL if it fails to allocate memory for the new string. 

