```rust
use tokio::sync::mpsc; // For the channel used for sending log messages from tasks to the main thread
use reqwest; // Library for handling HTTP requests in Rust
use std::time::Instant; // Library for timing how long a function takes to execute

// Struct representing a single log entry. Replace with your actual LogEntry struct.
struct LogEntry { /* ... */ }

async fn send_logs(tx: mpsc::Sender<LogEntry>) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let client = reqwest::Client::new(); // Create a new HTTP client.
    loop {
        match tx.recv().await {
            Ok(entry) => {
                let start = Instant::now();
                // Send the log entry to the remote service using the async `post` method from reqwest.
                if let Err(_) = client.post("https://remote-service.com/logs").json(&entry).send().await {
                    println_error("Failed to send logs to remote service.");
                    continue; // Retry sending the log entry if it failed.
                }
                let duration = start.elapsed();
                println_info(format_message("Sent log entry to remote service in {} milliseconds.", &duration));
            },
            Err(_) => {
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

This is a very basic example and would need to be extended with all the features you described. It's also important to note that writing such a large system in Rust alone would likely require many hours of work, even if we tried to make it as efficient as possible. You may want to consider using an existing logging solution designed for such systems like Fluentd or Logstash which can handle the majority of your needs out of the box and also integrate with Rust applications through libraries like `fluent-logger` in Rust.
