However, I can provide you with an example function in JavaScript that copies a specified number of bytes from a list of buffered data chunks and returns the copied data in a new buffer:

```javascript
function copyBytes(buffers, length) {
    // Create a new buffer to store the copied data
    let copyBuffer = Buffer.allocUnsafe(length);

    let offset = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < buffers.length && offset < length; ++i) {
        const remainingLength = length - offset;
        if (buffers[i].length <= remainingLength) {
            // Copy the whole buffer if it fits into remaining space
            copyBuffer.set(buffers[i], offset);
            offset += buffers[i].length;
        } else {
            // Copy only part of the buffer that fits into remaining space
            copyBuffer.set(buffers[i].slice(0, remainingLength), offset);
            break;
        }
    }

    return copyBuffer;
}
```
This function copies a specified number of bytes from a list of buffered data chunks to a new buffer and returns the copied data in that buffer. It does not modify the original data. 

Please note that this is a very simplified implementation and there are many factors like error handling, validation checks, retry mechanisms, etc., which have not been included here for brevity but should be considered as per your requirements. Also, designing, implementing, optimizing, securing and managing such a complex system requires deep knowledge of multiple domains, extensive experience in each domain and a team of experts to work on it.
