['Designing a distributed C library module with all these features is complex and beyond the scope of this platform. However, I can provide you an example of how to create a simple thread-safe collection using POSIX threads (pthreads) for concurrency handling and pthread_rwlock_t for read/write locking mechanism for data integrity:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#define MAX_SIZE 1000000 // Maximum size of the collection

typedef struct {
    int tag;
    void *data;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

// Global variables
NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T collection[MAX_SIZE]; // Our thread-safe collection
int count = 0; // Number of elements in the collection
pthread_rwlock_t lock = PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER; // Read/write lock for data integrity

void *add(void *data) {
    pthread_rwlock_wrlock(&lock); // Lock for writing

    if (count >= MAX_SIZE) return NULL; // Collection is full

    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T new_element;
    new_element.tag = rand(); // Random tag
    new_element.data = data; // Data to be added

    collection[count++] = new_element; // Add the element and increment count

    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&lock); // Unlock for writing
}

void *get(int tag) {
    void *data = NULL;

    pthread_rwlock_rdlock(&lock); // Lock for reading

    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) 
        if (collection[i].tag == tag) data = collection[i].data;

    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&lock); // Unlock for reading

    return data;
}
```
This example includes a basic add function to add an element to the collection and a get function to retrieve an element from the collection. It uses read/write locks to ensure thread safety, preventing race conditions and maintaining data integrity.

Note: This is just a simplified version of your task, for a complete solution you would need a lot more details about your requirements, such as what kind of distribution mechanisms you want to use (e.g., network-based or shared memory), how to handle irregular packet arrival patterns and varying network latencies, speculative execution, logging and error handling strategies, how to detect and recover from partitions/inconsistencies, the data storage strategies, performance profiling etc. I recommend breaking down your task into smaller tasks that you can tackle one by one.
