["The above task is highly complex and would require a lot of code to fully implement. However, I will outline a simplified example of how you could handle a batch processing service using Node.js with TypeScript. Please note that this is just the basic structure and does not cover all your requirements but it should give you a good starting point:

```typescript
import { EventEmitter } from 'events';
import DatabaseClient from './DatabaseClient'; // Assume we have an interface to interact with DB 
import ExternalLoggingService from './ExternalLoggingService';
import MetricsCollector from './MetricsCollector';

class EventProcessor extends EventEmitter {
    private batchSize: number = 10;
    private eventBuffer: Array<any> = []; // Assume events are just plain objects for simplicity
    private databaseClient: DatabaseClient;
    private loggingService: ExternalLoggingService;
    private metricsCollector: MetricsCollector;

    constructor() {
        super();
        this.databaseClient = new DatabaseClient();
        this.loggingService = new ExternalLoggingService();
        this.metricsCollector = new MetricsCollector();
    }

    public processEvent(event: any): void {
        this.eventBuffer.push(event);
        if (this.eventBuffer.length >= this.batchSize) {
            this.processBatch();
        } else {
            // Schedule a batch processing for the next tick of event loop to avoid blocking
            process.nextTick(() => this.processBatch());
        }
    }

    private async processBatch(): Promise<void> {
        if (this.eventBuffer.length === 0) return;

        const eventsToProcess = [...this.eventBuffer]; // Copy buffer to avoid race conditions
        this.eventBuffer = []; // Clear buffer

        try {
            await this.databaseClient.writeEvents(eventsToProcess);
            this.loggingService.log('Successfully processed batch of events', eventsToProcess);
        } catch (error) {
            console.error(`Error writing to database: ${error}`);
            // Retry with exponential backoff
            setTimeout(() => this.processBatch(), 1000);
            return;
        }

        this.metricsCollector.recordMetric('ProcessedEvents', eventsToProcess.length);
    }
}
```
This is a simplified example and doesn't handle horizontal scaling, thread safety, or other complexities that could be involved in a production system. Also, it doesn't include unit tests, integration tests, deployment strategy etc. However, I hope this gives you an idea of how you can start implementing your system using TypeScript and Node.js.
"]

