For SQL command construction in C#, you can utilize the `System.Data.SqlClient` namespace which provides a class called `SqlCommand`. Here's an example of how to dynamically construct a SQL command:

```csharp
public static string ConstructSQLQuery(string queryBase, List<object> parameters)
{
    StringBuilder sql = new StringBuilder();
    SqlCommand cmd = new SqlCommand(queryBase);

    for (int i = 0; i < parameters.Count; i++)
    {
        var parameterName = $"@p{i}";

        // Add the parameter to the command's Parameters collection.
        cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(parameterName, parameters[i]);

        if (sql.Length == 0)
            sql.Append($"EXEC {queryBase} ");

        sql.Append($"{parameterName}, ");
    }

    // Remove the trailing comma and space.
    sql.Remove(sql.Length - 2, 2);
    return sql.ToString();
}
```
In this example, we're assuming that `parameters` is a list of objects to be used as parameters in the SQL command. Note that you need to make sure to sanitize and validate any input before using it in an SQL query to prevent SQL injection attacks. 

