["Designing and implementing a real-time, distributed priority queue system in C++ that meets all of these requirements is quite extensive. Here's an abstract overview of how you might approach it:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <shared_mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

class PriorityQueue {
    std::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>> queue; // priority and value
    std::mutex mtx; // used for all operations except push
    std::shared_timed_mutex sharedMtx; // used only by push operation
    std::condition_variable cv; 

public:
    void push(int val, int priority) {
        std::unique_lock<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(sharedMtx);
        queue.push({priority, val});
        lock.unlock(); // unlock before notifying to reduce latency
        cv.notify_all();
    }

    int pop() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        while (queue.empty())
            cv.wait(lock);
        auto pair = queue.top(); 
        queue.pop();
        return pair.second; // value only
    }
};
```
This is a very basic priority queue implementation with thread-safe push and wait/notify mechanisms for pop operations.

A complete solution would require much more detailed design, including multiple server nodes, distributed memory management strategies, error handling, data format compatibility issues, integration with game engine API, etc. 

Note: This example is not a full library but it gives an idea about how one could be structured. A real-world implementation would need a lot of additional functionality and optimizations (caching, load balancing, memory management strategies, error handling, network handling, concurrent updates, data format compatibility issues, integration with game engine API, etc.). It's beyond the scope of this platform to provide a complete solution due to its complexity.
