['The task described is highly complex and involves a variety of programming skills including concurrent programming, file I/O operations, network programming, configuration management, data structures, and more. 

Due to the complexity involved, it would be very hard to produce a code block that implements all these features in a single answer. However, here's an example on how you can start implementing such a library. Note that this is a simplified pseudo-code representation of your requirements:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>

// Globally accessible mutex for thread safety
pthread_mutex_t lock; 

typedef struct {
    char *directory;
    int maxSeqNum;
} Config;

Config config;

void init(char* directory, int maxSeqNum) {
    pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL);

    // Configure the global namespace. For example:
    char *dir = "/var/lib/myapp";
    mkdir(dir, 0755);

    config.directory = strdup(directory);
    config.maxSeqNum = maxSeqNum;
}

char* generateUniqueFilename() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    DIR *dirp = opendir(config.directory);
    struct dirent *dp;

    int seq = 0;

    // Find the highest sequence number in use
    while ((dp = readdir(dirp)) != NULL) {
        if (strcmp(dp->d_name, ".") == 0 || strcmp(dp->d_name, "..") == 0) continue;

        int num = 0;
        sscanf(dp->d_name, "%*[^0-9]%d", &num);

        if (num > seq) {
            seq = num;
        }
    }

    closedir(dirp);

    // Increment the sequence number and check for overflow
    seq++;
    if (seq > config.maxSeqNum) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return NULL;
    }

    char *filename = malloc(strlen(config.directory) + 10); // Assuming 4 digit seq number
    sprintf(filename, "%s/file%04d", config.directory, seq);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return filename;
}
```
This code demonstrates how to create a globally accessible mutex that will be used for ensuring thread safety in the generateUniqueFilename() function. It iterates over the directory and finds the highest sequence number, increments it, and checks if it exceeds maxSeqNum. If not, it creates a new filename by appending this sequence number to the directory path and returns it. 

Remember, implementing such complex system requires an in-depth understanding of different concepts like thread safety, file I/O operations, synchronization primitives, error handling, configuration management etc. This is just the tip of the iceberg and actual implementation may involve more sophisticated strategies and techniques based on specific use cases, requirements, and constraints.
