["This is quite an extensive task, here's a simplified version of what a thread-safe queue could look like in C++. However, note that actual implementation would be complex and depend on specific requirements. 

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

class ThreadSafeQueue {
public:
    explicit ThreadSafeQueue(size_t capacity) : maxSize_(capacity) {}

    void enqueue(int data) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);

        cond_.wait(lock, [this](){ return queue_.size() < maxSize_; });

        queue_.push(data);
        lock.unlock();
        cond_.notify_one();
    }

    int dequeue() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);

        cond_.wait(lock, [this](){ return !queue_.empty(); });

        auto data = queue_.front();
        queue_.pop();
        lock.unlock();
        cond_.notify_one();

        return data;
    }

    size_t size() const {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        return queue_.size();
    }

private:
    mutable std::mutex mtx_;
    std::condition_variable cond_;
    std::queue<int> queue_;
    size_t maxSize_;
};
```
This class creates a thread-safe queue that can be safely accessed from multiple threads. It uses `std::unique_lock` to manage access to the queue, along with `std::condition_variable` and `std::mutex` for waiting if the queue is full or empty. 

Please note, this is a highly simplified example. In a real-world scenario, you would need to consider additional factors such as exception safety, thread safety in different scenarios, optimization (e.g., lock striping), etc. 

