That being said, I can give you an example of how you could start designing the basic structure of your application, but this is just a simple skeleton and does not cover all your requirements:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <shared_mutex>
#include <memory>
#include <mutex>
#include <string>

class KeyValueStore {
public:
    void set(const std::string& key, const std::string& value) {
        std::unique_lock<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(_accessMutex);
        _data[key] = value;
    }

    std::string get(const std::string& key) {
        std::shared_lock<std::shared_timed_mutex> lock(_accessMutex);
        auto it = _data.find(key);
        if (it != _data.end()) {
            return it->second;
        } else {
            return "";  // Or throw an exception, depending on your needs
        }
    }

private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> _data;
    mutable std::shared_timed_mutex _accessMutex;
};
```
This is a very basic thread-safe key-value store. It uses a shared timed mutex (`std::shared_timed_mutex`) to provide both exclusive and shared access to the data, while `std::unique_lock` provides exclusive access during writes, and `std::shared_lock` for reads.

Note: Implementing your requirements would require a significant amount of code that you have not specified (such as distributed systems, caching strategies, machine learning-based eviction policies, reliable notification system, logging framework, flexible version control, query interfaces, integration with other systems), and each one of these can be complex and large in their own right. 

