        sampleRate(rate), channelCount(count), bitDepth(depth), rawAudioBuffer(nullptr) {
            // Add validation logic here. Throw exception if inputs are invalid.
    }
};
```
**Dynamic Buffer Management:**
We can use a lock-free queue for high performance scenarios, and traditional heap allocation for lower-latency situations. Here's a simple implementation of a lock-free ring buffer using `std::array`:
```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <array>

template<typename T, size_t N>
class RingBuffer {
    std::array<T, N> buf;
    std::atomic<size_t> head = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);
    std::atomic<size_t> tail = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);

public:
    bool push(const T& data) {
        auto currentTail = tail.load();
        if ((currentTail + 1) % N == head.load()) { // full
            return false; 
        } else {
            buf[currentTail] = data;
            tail.store((currentTail + 1) % N); 
            return true;
        }
    }

    bool pop(T& result) {
        auto currentHead = head.load();
        if (currentHead == tail.load()) { // empty
            return false;
        } else {
            result = buf[currentHead];
            head.store((currentHead + 1) % N); 
            return true;
        }
    }
};
```
**Real-Time Processing Pipeline:**
This would involve integrating with external APIs, and would likely involve creating classes for each API you're interacting with. For example:
```cpp
class AudioStream {
public:
    virtual bool start() = 0; // Start pulling/pushing audio data
    virtual void stop() = 0;  // Stop pulling/pushing audio data
};
```
The specific implementation would depend on the API you're interfacing with. You would need to handle error cases, and potentially retry operations if they fail.

