['This task requires a comprehensive understanding of C++ programming, concurrent programming in C++, and advanced design patterns for efficient resource management. This is a complex task that involves creating a dynamic collection manager with real-time integration and error handling features. Due to the lengthy nature of this task, I can only provide a high-level structure of such library component in C++14 or later. 

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <atomic>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <queue>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <iostream>

// Define the CELL_EVENT_RECORD struct here.
struct CELL_EVENT_RECORD {
    int CELL_ID;
    double EVENT_TIMESTAMP;
    std::string EVENT_TYPE;
    double MEASUREMENT_DATA;
};

class EventCollectionManager {
public:
    EventCollectionManager(size_t initialCapacity = 1024) : capacity_(initialCapacity), size_(0) {}

    ~EventCollectionManager() {
        for (auto& rec : records_) delete rec.second;
    }

    void addRecord(CELL_EVENT_RECORD* record) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        while (size_.load() >= capacity_) {
            // Backpressure mechanism
            conditionVariable_.wait(lock);
        }
        records_[record->CELL_ID] = record;
        ++size_;
    }

    void removeRecord(int cellId) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto it = records_.find(cellId);
        if (it != records_.end()) {
            delete it->second;
            records_.erase(it);
            --size_;
        }
    }

    CELL_EVENT_RECORD* getRecord(int cellId) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        auto it = records_.find(cellId);
        if (it != records_.end()) return it->second;
        else return nullptr;
    }

private:
    // A thread safe collection is needed here. 
    std::unordered_map<int, CELL_EVENT_RECORD*> records_;

    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable conditionVariable_;

    std::atomic<size_t> size_;
    const size_t capacity_;
};
```
This code provides a basic skeleton for an EventCollectionManager class that manages a collection of CELL_EVENT_RECORD objects. It implements a thread-safe add, remove and get operations on the records_. It uses std::mutex to ensure exclusive access to shared data, which is size_, and records_ collections. A backpressure mechanism is implemented with conditionVariable_ in addRecord function.

For implementing more advanced features like error handling, scalability, configuration options etc., it would be necessary to expand this code further. For example, you may need to design an async task dispatcher for better concurrency management and introduce a memory pooling technique to optimize for memory usage. This is just a start point.
