```java
// Importing required classes
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class GatewayConfig {
    @Bean
    public RouteLocator gatewayRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
                .route(r -> r.path("/payments/**")
                        .filters(f -> f.circuitBreaker(config -> 
                        	config.setName("paymentCB").setFallbackUri("forward:/fallback/payments")))
                        .uri("lb://PAYMENT-SERVICE"))

                .route(r -> r.path("/users/**")
                        .filters(f -> f.circuitBreaker(config -> 
                        	config.setName("userCB").setFallbackUri("forward:/fallback/users")))
                        .uri("lb://USER-SERVICE"))

                // Add other routes as per your requirements...

                .build();
    }
}
```
In the above code, we're using Spring Cloud Gateway to create dynamic routes. Each route is associated with a circuit breaker that will trigger if the service fails after three consecutive failures. Also, for each route failure, it will redirect to `/fallback/{serviceName}` endpoint which should be implemented by you according to your application needs.

Please note that above code snippet just demonstrates how to setup dynamic routing with Spring Cloud Gateway but doesn't cover other requirements like security (like mutual TLS authentication), performance optimization (like caching, async processing etc) and error handling, which require a more detailed understanding of Java-based system design.
